    "id": "news-1792258711461-2",
    "title": "Clashes and floods compound humanitarian crisis in South West Somalia",
    "original_title": "Koonfur Galbeed: Dagaal, Barakac iyo Daadad Isbiirsaday oo Halis ku Haya Boqollaal Kun oo Shacab ah",
    "slug": "clashes-and-floods-compound-humanitarian-crisis-in-south-west-somalia",
    "summary": "Fighting between forces loyal to former South West President Laftagareen and federal troops has spread across Bay region, displacing thousands and complicating flood response.",
    "content": "Fighting between forces loyal to former South West State President Abdiasis Hassan Mohamed (Laftagareen) and federal troops has expanded beyond Baidoa to several towns and villages in the Bay region, according to reports confirmed by Goobjoog News. Laftagareen's office stated that his militia has taken control of the four districts of Qansahdhere, Burhakaba, Bardale and Dinsoor, as well as the Awdiinle area. The development marks a significant widening of a conflict that had initially been concentrated in Baidoa and now threatens to destabilize a broader swath of southern Somalia.\n\nThe clashes come amid a political standoff between Laftagareen and the federal government in Mogadishu over the composition of South West State's administration. The former leader, who has been in dispute with federal authorities, has reportedly mobilized clan militias to assert control over territory. The fighting has already severely affected civilians, disrupting movement and humanitarian operations in areas that were already vulnerable.\n\nBay region hosts a large concentration of internally displaced persons, many of whom have fled previous droughts and conflict. The new wave of violence has forced additional families to flee their homes, often into already overcrowded camps with limited access to shelter, food and healthcare. Aid agencies have warned that access to affected populations is becoming increasingly difficult as frontlines shift.\n\nThe situation is further compounded by seasonal floods that have hit parts of South West State, blocking roads and isolating communities. The combination of conflict and flooding has created a dual crisis, with humanitarian convoys unable to reach those most in need. Local officials and aid workers say the overlapping shocks are stretching already limited resources to the breaking point.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the escalation is deeply concerning. Many diaspora members have family in Baidoa and the wider Bay region and regularly send remittances that support relatives and local projects. The fighting threatens to disrupt these lifelines and could trigger a new wave of displacement, both internally and across borders. Diaspora groups have historically played a key role in funding clinics, schools and food distributions, but access constraints may hamper their ability to assist.\n\nObservers warn that if the conflict continues to spread, it could undermine recent gains in state-building and humanitarian access in the region. Calls for de-escalation and dialogue have so far gone unheeded, and the humanitarian community is urging all parties to allow safe passage for aid. The coming weeks will be critical in determining whether the situation stabilizes or deteriorates further, with ordinary civilians bearing the brunt of the crisis.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/17/koonfur-galbeed-dagaal-barakac-iyo-daadad-isbiirsaday-oo-halis-ku-haya-boqollaal-kun-oo-shacab-ah/",
